fact WorkItem(id: "WI-645", description: some(value: "BUG (soundness) + DESIGN: Anthill's `Eq` CONFLATES a partial equality (`PartialEq`: may be non-reflexive) with a lawful equivalence (`Eq`: reflexive), and the resolver/interpreter HARDCODES reflexivity -- which is unsound for ANY partial carrier. Float (NaN) is the first such carrier: the interpreter's semantic Float `eq`/`neq` and `Ordered.gt/lt/gte/lte` violate IEEE 754 and DIVERGE from both the C++ codegen and the stdlib's own contract. Same Anthill program, OPPOSITE answers interpreted vs compiled.\n\nEMPIRICALLY CONFIRMED (interpreter, `nan` = anthill.prelude.Float.nan):\n  eq(nan, nan)  = true   [IEEE + stdlib say FALSE] -- BUG   |  gt(nan, 1.0) = true  [IEEE FALSE] -- BUG\n  neq(nan, nan) = false  [IEEE + stdlib say TRUE]  -- BUG   |  lt(nan, 1.0) = false [coincidentally right]\n  nan === nan   = true   [structural identity -- CORRECT, keep]\nThe gt=true/lt=false asymmetry is the \"NaN sorts as OrderedFloat's MAX\" fingerprint.\n\nROOT CAUSE IS NOT A LAW -- IT IS HARDCODED REFLEXIVITY. The `Eq` sort (stdlib/anthill/prelude/eq.anthill) declares only `rule neq(?a,?b) <=> not(eq(?a,?b))`, which is IEEE-CONSISTENT (neq(nan,nan)=not(false)=true). There is NO reflexivity/symmetry/transitivity law anywhere in the stdlib. The IEEE violation comes from the IMPLEMENTATION assuming `eq(x,x)=true`: (1) sem_eq_core's fast-path `if values_equal(a,b) return true` (resolve.rs:3493) and (2) eval `builtin_eq` = views_structurally_equal (all-structural, eval/builtins.rs:417). Both read Float leaves as Literal::Float(OrderedFloat<f64>) (term_view.rs:793, term.rs:282), and ordered_float treats \"all NaN equal\" + \"NaN is the max\" (ordered-float lib.rs:84,294). Ordered.gt/lt = builtin_cmp reads value_num->OrderedFloat, so the total order (NaN=max) leaks into comparisons too.\n\nTHE DISTINCTION IS PartialEq vs Eq (Rust precedent). Rust gives `f64: PartialEq` (== is IEEE, NaN != NaN, reflexivity NOT guaranteed) but `f64: !Eq` (Eq is the stricter reflexive MARKER, which f64 deliberately does not implement BECAUSE of NaN). Anthill's single `Eq` merges the two and the resolver silently assumes the strong (reflexive) one -- so IEEE `==` (lawfully only `PartialEq`) cannot be a lawful `Eq` instance. This is also adjacent to the WI-573 hazard the sem_eq_core doc already flags (structural fast-path ignoring a carrier's own `eq`).\n\nDIVERGES FROM CODEGEN: anthill-cpp-gen maps Eq.eq->C++ `==`, Ordered.gt->`>` (lib.rs:2902/2898). C++ double ==/> are genuine IEEE, so COMPILED eq(nan,nan)=false, gt(nan,1.0)=false -- the opposite of the interpreter. And CONTRADICTS THE STDLIB CONTRACT: float.anthill:19-20 \"Float.eq returns false for NaN (NaN != NaN in IEEE)\"; :59 \"nan is unequal to everything, itself included\".\n\nLOAD-BEARING CONSTRAINT: OrderedFloat is REQUIRED at the STRUCTURAL layer (Literal must be Hash+Eq for TermStore hash-consing/dedup; `===`/struct_eq is structural identity and nan===nan=true is CORRECT). So the STRUCTURAL layer (===, views_structurally_equal, Literal-Hash) must stay OrderedFloat; only the SEMANTIC path changes.\n\nTWO FIX DIRECTIONS:\n  (A) PRAGMATIC INTERIM -- treat `Eq` as PartialEq-like: DROP/guard the hardcoded reflexivity for partial carriers so semantic Eq/Ordered on Float read raw f64 IEEE (eq(nan,_)=false, neq=true, all NaN-operand comparisons false, -0.0 eq +0.0 = true), matching codegen. sem_eq_core's `values_equal` reflexivity fast-path must NOT short-circuit two NaNs; eval builtin_eq needs the Float-numeric IEEE path (builtin_cmp already extracts value_num for Ordered -- eq/neq need the analogous path, falling back to structural for non-numeric so Set/Map/entity eq is unchanged; WI-616 carrier-override dispatch preserved). Keeps `===` structural. Fast, unblocks the interp/codegen soundness divergence, but leaves a non-reflexive thing still named `Eq`.\n  (B) PRINCIPLED TARGET (likely its own design ticket, supersedes WI-644's \"drop the default\"): SPLIT the hierarchy PartialEq subset-of Eq -- `eq`/`neq` on a PartialEq-like spec (Float provides it, IEEE); a reflexive `Eq`/`TotalEq` MARKER Float does NOT provide, REQUIRED by dedup / set keys / sorting / simp-reflexivity; a total-Float wrapper (OrderedFloat-style) for reflexive contexts; and the \"modular symbol loading\" question -- keep IEEE `==` a distinct symbol from lawful `eq`, resolved per module/import so the two never conflate. Codegen: PartialEq.eq -> C++ ==; reflexive contexts use the total wrapper.\n\nRELATION: standalone SOUNDNESS bug (interp != codegen != documented contract); does NOT depend on the SLD->eval bridge (WI-625 -- the culprit is the raw interpreter builtins + resolver fast-path). Concrete evidence that Float is a primitive whose host equality is NON-structural, so it needs PartialEq semantics, not a lawful/reflexive Eq -- informs WI-644 (Eq-instance semantics) and builds on WI-616 (semantic eq dispatch).\n\nACCEPTANCE (direction A, interim): interpreter eq(nan,nan)=false, neq(nan,nan)=true, gt/lt/gte/lte with a NaN operand = false (IEEE unordered), -0.0 eq +0.0 = true; the resolver's reflexivity fast-path no longer forces `eq(x,x)` for a partial (Float) carrier; nan === nan stays true (structural identity, unchanged); Set/Map/entity structural eq + hash-consing/dedup unchanged; the same ops codegen'd to C++ agree with the interpreter; repro test wi645_float_nan_ieee_test.rs un-ignored; cargo test green. (Direction B is tracked as the follow-on design ticket if chosen.) REF: eval/builtins.rs:413-422, kb/resolve.rs (sem_eq_core reflexivity fast-path ~3493 / values_equal / builtin_cmp / value_num), kb/term_view.rs:780-823, kb/term.rs:278-282, stdlib/anthill/prelude/eq.anthill (no reflexivity law), stdlib/anthill/prelude/float.anthill:19-62, anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs:2889-2906. Rust-only (interpreter + resolver; scaland shares the OrderedFloat structural comparator -- mirror if it exposes semantic eq)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-09T13:18:51Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-645", author: "user", content: "Principled resolution filed: library proposal 004 (PartialEq/Eq/PartialOrd/Ord split) + driver WI-644 -- this is the ticket's DIRECTION B. library-004 build-step 2 IS the fix (gate sem_eq_core's reflexivity fast-path to reflexive-Eq carriers; eval builtin_eq IEEE-for-Float) and un-ignores the wi645 repro test. The interim direction A (make Float eq IEEE without the spec split) is unnecessary if library-004 lands, since the split is what JUSTIFIES the IEEE answer (Float provides PartialEq, not lawful Eq). Prior art confirmed against Lean: it withholds LawfulBEq Float for non-reflexivity AND non-congruence (-0.0).", at: "2026-07-05T21:22:13Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-645", author: "user", content: "ALREADY DELIVERED via WI-644 (Direction B, the principled PartialEq/Eq + PartialOrd/Ordered split) + WI-664 (composite field-wise eq) -- no separate implementation needed, exactly as this ticket's prior feedback anticipated ('library-004 build-step 2 IS the fix ... and un-ignores the wi645 repro test'; direction A unnecessary once the split lands). VERIFIED 2026-07-09 on clean main (HEAD 720e3c04), zero code change in this delivery. Repro test wi645_float_nan_ieee_test: 3 passed, 0 ignored -- eq_ordered_on_nan_follow_ieee (IEEE target), struct_eq_on_nan_stays_structural, totalfloat_eq_is_lawful_reflexive_on_nan. Acceptance confirmed against current code: eval float_ieee_eq = raw f64 == (eq(nan,nan)=false, neq=true, -0.0 eq +0.0 = true); resolver sem_eq_core gates on value_f64 BEFORE the reflexivity shortcut; builtin_cmp uses partial_cmp so gt/lt/gte/lte with a NaN operand = false (IEEE unordered); === stays on views_structurally_equal (nan===nan=true, hash-consing/dedup intact); Set/Map/entity structural eq unchanged. WI-664 covers the composite level (Point(nan,_) no longer laundered). scaland: no mirror needed (loads no semantic eq). Full workspace green: 69 binaries, 2665 passed, 0 failed.", at: "2026-07-09T13:18:50Z")
